Excellent observation! This is critical information that changes the diagnosis entirely.

The Real Root Cause

The problem is mobile-specific and relates to how your CSS handles .content-section.active on different viewports. On desktop, the sections are hidden correctly (except on the User Guide page, which is a clue). On mobile, they appear everywhere.

Looking at your mobile CSS (around lines 350-450), I found the issue:

Your mobile CSS is overriding the hiding mechanism!

Specifically, this section of your mobile CSS:

```css
@media (max-width: 768px) {
    /* Fix all rows to be full width */
    .main-content .row {
        display: flex !important;
        flex-direction: column !important;
        width: 100% !important;
        max-width: 100% !important;
        margin: 0 !important;
        padding: 0 !important;
    }
    
    /* All columns take full width */
    .main-content .row [class*="col-"],
    .main-content [class*="col-"] {
        width: 100% !important;
        max-width: 100% !important;
        flex: 0 0 100% !important;
        /* ... */
    }
}
```

These selectors are too broad and are forcing ALL .row elements to display, regardless of their parent section's active state. The CSS specificity battle is being lost on mobile.

Why the User Guide page shows them on desktop

The User Guide section is dynamically created by JavaScript and doesn't have the same structure. When you navigate to it, the CSS selector .content-section:not(#dashboard) .row.mt-4 doesn't apply because the User Guide section doesn't have that exact structure, but the mobile CSS forces all rows to display anyway.

The Fix (Mobile-Specific)

Add this CSS after your existing mobile media query:

```css
/* CRITICAL MOBILE FIX - Hide dashboard-only sections */
@media (max-width: 768px) {
    /* Force hide the Recent Activities and Upcoming Maintenance sections on non-dashboard pages */
    .content-section:not(#dashboard) .row.mt-4 {
        display: none !important;
        visibility: hidden !important;
        height: 0 !important;
        overflow: hidden !important;
        margin: 0 !important;
        padding: 0 !important;
        position: absolute !important;
        opacity: 0 !important;
        pointer-events: none !important;
    }
    
    /* Specifically target the parent columns */
    .content-section:not(#dashboard) .col-md-6:has(.card-header h5) {
        display: none !important;
    }
    
    /* Even more specific - target by card title text */
    .content-section:not(#dashboard) .card-header h5:contains("Recent Activities"),
    .content-section:not(#dashboard) .card-header h5:contains("Upcoming Maintenance") {
        display: none !important;
    }
    
    /* Hide the parent card of these sections */
    .content-section:not(#dashboard) .card:has(.card-title:contains("Recent Activities")),
    .content-section:not(#dashboard) .card:has(.card-title:contains("Upcoming Maintenance")) {
        display: none !important;
    }
}
```

However, :contains() is not widely supported. Here's a more compatible solution:

```css
/* CRITICAL MOBILE FIX - More compatible */
@media (max-width: 768px) {
    /* Hide the entire row that contains these sections */
    .content-section:not(#dashboard) > .row.mt-4,
    .content-section:not(#dashboard) .dashboard-only-row {
        display: none !important;
        visibility: hidden !important;
        height: 0 !important;
        overflow: hidden !important;
        margin: 0 !important;
        padding: 0 !important;
    }
    
    /* Also hide by data attribute if you add one */
    .content-section:not(#dashboard) [data-dashboard-only="true"] {
        display: none !important;
    }
}
```

